# .surface-breaking — the changelog of surface we have deliberately TAKEN AWAY.
#
# scripts/check-surface.sh fails on any fact that disappears from the surface,
# because someone out there has it in a script. To remove one on purpose, paste
# the exact `.surface` line here under a dated comment saying why, then run
# `scripts/check-surface.sh --update`.
#
# Two rules keep this file honest:
#   * an entry only counts while the line is genuinely gone. Listing a line that
#     still exists is an error (a stale acknowledgement would pre-authorise a
#     future removal nobody reviewed).
#   * entries are never deleted after the fact — this file is the record of what
#     a user's old script may be relying on.
#
# Format: one verbatim .surface line per row.
#
# 2026-07-28 — nothing removed yet.

# 2026-07-28 — two notes REPLACED because they were false, not because the
# surface went away. Both are restated in the same column, corrected:
#
#   * the %global note claimed `--agent` wraps the response and `--json` never
#     changes shape. A `--batch` run emits bare JSONL under `--agent` and, on
#     failure, exits 3 with no {"ok":false} at all; and `--fields` projects and
#     re-serialises `.data` through jq. The skill already said the batch part
#     correctly, so the CLI's own docs contradicted each other.
#   * the tasks note said "neither resolution happens ... for an argument that
#     already looks like an id". That is now true only of a READ: a WRITE
#     probes an id-shaped argument for an exact name match and refuses a
#     collision (--by-id / --by-name decide).
note %global `--agent` wraps the response in {ok,data,meta,summary,breadcrumbs}; `--json` stays the RAW API body and never changes
note tasks neither resolution happens on a write, in a `--batch`, or for an argument that already looks like an id

# 2026-08-03 — BEHAVIOUR REMOVED, no fact removed. Recorded here anyway.
#
# No `.surface` line changed, so check-surface.sh is silent about this and
# always would be: the surface table describes commands and flags, and what
# changed is what the CLI DOES when nobody asked it to do anything. That is a
# blind spot in the ratchet, not an absence of breakage — hence this note.
#
# uku no longer installs updates by itself (C1).
#
#   was:  once a day, on almost any command, a newer VERSION triggered
#         cmd_update → `curl -fsSL <url> | sh`, unattended.
#   now:  the same daily check prints one line naming the new version and
#         `uku update`, and installs nothing.
#
# UKU_NO_AUTO_UPDATE=1 keeps its meaning — it now silences the notice and skips
# the check, so anyone who already set it sees no change at all.
#
# Who this can break: a machine that relied on picking up releases with no
# human in the loop (CI images, agent sandboxes) now stays on its installed
# version until something runs `uku update`. That is the intended cost. The
# unattended path fetched a remote script and piped it into `sh` on a machine
# holding a live API key, with every integrity control living inside the
# artefact being fetched — which cannot protect the choice of artefact.
#
# Also changed, same release, same reason:
#   * UKU_INSTALL_URL_DEFAULT no longer resolves through getuku.com; it is
#     pinned to an immutable tag on the repo. A third-party deploy is no longer
#     in the path of code this CLI executes.
#   * UKU_UPDATE_URL_DEFAULT deliberately still tracks main. It is read and
#     compared, never executed, and pinning it would end update detection.
#
# Pinned by tests/cases/update-trust.sh §0, §6 and §7.

# 2026-08-03 — the --agent envelope gained a "warnings" key (C5), so the note
# enumerating its shape is REPLACED, not withdrawn. The restated line is in
# .surface with warnings named between meta and summary.
#
# The API returns a non-fatal warnings[] on a 2xx — TIME_ENTRY_OVERLAP,
# MONITOR_OVERLAP, AGREEMENT_OVERLAP — meaning "this was accepted, and there is
# something about it you should know". The CLI read .data and .meta and dropped
# warnings on the floor, so a person booking time against a task was never told
# it overlapped one they already had.
#
# Adding a key to a JSON object does not break a consumer that indexes by name,
# which is every sane consumer. It is recorded here because the note PROMISED
# an exact shape, and a promise about shape is the kind of thing someone writes
# a schema against.
note %global `--agent` wraps the response in {ok,data,meta,summary,breadcrumbs} — EXCEPT a `--batch` run, which stays JSONL one object per input line under `--agent`, and whose failures are those lines, so a failed run exits 3 with no {"ok":false} envelope at all; `--json` stays the RAW API body, except that `--fields` projects each row and re-serialises it through jq

# 2026-08-04 — OUTPUT SHAPE CHANGED, no fact removed. Recorded anyway (C12).
#
# `uku --help --agent` no longer carries per-command `notes`. Nothing was
# withdrawn: `uku <command> --help --agent` returns the same notes it always
# did, in full, for one command. The top level became an index.
#
#   was:  14621 bytes, 1.44x the size of the human --help
#   now:   7217 bytes, 0.69x — and 7622 of the bytes removed were notes
#
# Why it counts as a break even though no .surface line moved: the PRIME
# DIRECTIVE names OUTPUT SHAPES as contract, and a consumer doing
# `uku --help --agent | jq '.commands[] | select(.command=="tasks") | .notes'`
# now gets null. The fix for such a consumer is one call:
# `uku tasks --help --agent | jq .notes`.
#
# The new arrangement is itself declared as a %global note, so it is ratcheted
# rather than merely true today, and the payload names it in a `more` field so
# an agent reading top-to-bottom learns there is more before it consumes
# twenty command cards.

# 2026-08-04 — C6. The exit-code TAXONOMY changed. Nothing was removed from
# .surface (the codes were not declared there until today — that was the bug
# that let this change be invisible to the ratchet), so there is no verbatim
# line to paste. This entry is the record instead, and it is a real break:
#
#   HTTP 403        exit 2 (auth)       -> exit 7 (forbidden)
#   HTTP 400/422    exit 3 (api)        -> exit 8 (validation)
#   HTTP 429        exit 5 (network)    -> exit 9 (ratelimit)
#   spent local write budget, single and --batch
#                   exit 5 (network)    -> exit 9 (ratelimit)
#   MISSING_COMPANY / INVALID_COMPANY (an HTTP 400)
#                   exit 3 (api)        -> exit 2 (auth)
#
# In the --agent envelope the `code` field moves with them: "auth" ->
# "forbidden" on a 403, "api" -> "validation" on a 400/422.
#
# Why, given the PRIME DIRECTIVE: agents branch on $?. Collapsed, exit 2 told an
# agent to re-authenticate when the key was fine and merely lacked a scope, and
# exit 3 told it "your data was wrong" for MISSING_COMPANY — so it retried with
# different data forever. Exit 5 meant "retry now" for a rate limit, where the
# right move is to wait for the reset. Every one of those is a wrong next action
# a script cannot recover from by reading prose.
#
# A script that branched on the OLD codes still works for the cases it handled
# unless it treated 2/3/5 as exhaustive. Ratcheted from now on: the codes are
# `exit N name` facts in .surface, so the next change cannot be silent.
